# XSH timer_create()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Timers use SIGRTMIN, SIGUSR1 and sigtimedwait(): the signal is blocked and given a do-nothing
# SA_SIGINFO action, so that an expiration is queued with its value. Time bounds are lower
# bounds only (an expiration is never early; allowed to be short by one step of the clock's
# resolution when measured); the upper bounds are hang guards. EAGAIN (out of signal-queuing
# resources or timers) cannot be provoked portably. CPU-time clocks of other processes and
# threads, the default signal number for a null evp, and a fixed thread stack address for
# SIGEV_THREAD are not tested.

assertion  XSH.timer_create.001
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       timer_create() on CLOCK_REALTIME and on CLOCK_MONOTONIC, with a null
           evp and with a SIGEV_NONE evp, returns 0 and the timer is disarmed:
           timer_gettime() reports a zero time until expiration.
note       All implementations support these two clocks (the monotonic
           requirement is from 2024).
test       t/001-clocks-disarmed.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.002
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Eight timers created together have eight different timer IDs.
test       t/002-unique-ids.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.003
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2001
says       timer_create() with clock IDs 1000000 and 0x3fffffff, which no clock
           has, returns -1 with EINVAL.
note       The values are chosen to be outside any clock numbering in use; a
           system that defines one of them would make this test fail wrongly.
test       t/003-einval-clock.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.004
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a null evp the timer, when it expires, generates a signal for the
           process (any signal; all signals are blocked and one is accepted
           within 3 s).
note       The default signal number is not specified, so any signal is accepted
           (every signal gets a do-nothing handler first, so that none is ignored
           by default while blocked). The value delivered is not checked.
test       t/004-null-evp.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.005
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2001
says       A timer with SIGEV_SIGNAL, signal number SIGRTMIN (and again SIGUSR1)
           and value 12345 makes that signal available at expiration with
           si_signo the chosen number, si_code SI_TIMER and si_value 12345.
test       t/005-sigev-signal.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.006
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2001
says       A timer with SIGEV_NONE that has expired generated no signal (none of
           the signals, all blocked, is accepted within 400 ms of an expiration
           due at 50 ms), and is disarmed afterwards.
note       "Disarmed afterwards" is the reading of a one-shot timer that has
           expired as one with no time left to expiration.
test       t/006-sigev-none.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.007
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.1
class      required
since      2001
says       A timer with SIGEV_THREAD calls sigev_notify_function with the
           sigev_value (4711) at expiration, in a thread other than the one that
           created the timer.
note       The wait on the pipe uses a poll(); the helper is shared with the
           other timer tests.
test       t/007-sigev-thread.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.008
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A child made by fork() while a timer is armed does not receive the
           timer's expiration signal (blocked signal not accepted within 1.5 s),
           while the parent does.
test       t/008-fork-not-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.009
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A timer armed to signal SIGUSR1 (default action: terminate) in 100 ms
           does not do so after the process has executed another program that
           lives for a second.
test       t/009-exec-disarms.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.010
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       CPT
since      2001
says       timer_create() on CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID returns 0, the timer
           expires once the process has used the CPU time asked for (20 ms;
           waited for with a busy loop bounded to 15 s) and not before.
test       t/010-cputime-process.c

assertion  XSH.timer_create.011
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       TCT
since      2001
says       timer_create() on CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID returns 0, the timer expires
           once the calling thread has used the CPU time asked for (20 ms; busy
           loop bounded to 15 s) and not before.
test       t/011-cputime-thread.c
